  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Ich nehme lieber die höhere Wahrscheinlichkeitssumme (also 25%) als den höheren Geldbetrag, weil die Chancen höher sind.' This translates to: 'I prefer the higher probability sum (i.e., 25%) over the higher monetary amount because the chances are higher.' The individual focuses on the probability of receiving a positive outcome (25% for A vs. 20% for B), and explicitly chooses based on the higher probability. There is no mention of skewness, distribution of outcomes, or any consideration of the shape of the probability distribution. The justification is based solely on the magnitude of the probability of a non-zero outcome, not on the skewness of the lottery. The individual does not refer to favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes in terms of probability distribution or asymmetry. Thus, the deliberation process centers on the probability of success, not on the skewness of the outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual evaluates only the magnitude of the probability of a favorable outcome (25% vs. 20%), without analyzing the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. The decision reason requires assessing whether one lottery has more probability mass on favorable outcomes (i.e., higher monetary payoff) than on unfavorable (zero) outcomes — which would indicate left-skewness. While A has a higher probability of a positive outcome, the reason requires a comparison of the distribution's asymmetry, not just the total probability. The individual’s justification is based on a different principle — the higher probability of a non-zero outcome — which is not equivalent to assessing skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The reasoning is conceptually distinct and does not reflect the rule of preferring more left-skewed lotteries.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
